+## `jj git ref`
+
+Manage Git refs
+
+**Usage:** `jj git ref <COMMAND>`
+
+###### **Subcommands:**
+
+* `fetch` — Fetch a ref from a Git remote
+* `list` — List fetched Git refs
+* `forget` — Forget a fetched Git ref
+
+
+
+## `jj git ref fetch`
+
+Fetch a ref from a Git remote
+
+If a working-copy commit gets abandoned, it will be given a new, empty commit. This is true in general; it is not specific to this command.
+
+**Usage:** `jj git ref fetch [OPTIONS] <REF>`
+
+###### **Arguments:**
+
+* `<REF>` — The ref to fetch
+
+###### **Options:**
+
+* `--remote <REMOTE>` — The remote to fetch from (only named remotes are supported)
+
+   This defaults to the first remote in the `git.fetch` setting. If that is not configured, and if there are multiple remotes, the remote named "origin" will be used.
+* `--new` — Create and edit a new empty commit on the fetched ref
+* `--edit` — Edit the fetched commit directly
+* `--bookmark <NAME>` — Set a local bookmark to the fetched commit
+* `--replace` — Allow --bookmark to move an existing local bookmark
+
+
+
+## `jj git ref list`
+
+List fetched Git refs
+
+**Usage:** `jj git ref list [OPTIONS] [REF]`
+
+###### **Arguments:**
+
+* `<REF>` — Show this fetched ref
+
+###### **Options:**
+
+* `--remote <REMOTE>` — Show refs fetched from this remote
+
+
+
+## `jj git ref forget`
+
+Forget a fetched Git ref
+
+**Usage:** `jj git ref forget --remote <REMOTE> <REF>`
+
+###### **Arguments:**
+
+* `<REF>` — The fetched ref to forget
+
+###### **Options:**
+
+* `--remote <REMOTE>` — Forget a ref fetched from this remote
+
+
+
 ## `jj git push`
 
 Push to a Git remote
